plasma-camera: zoom bar, with an automatic crossover to the ultra-wide

WHAT IT DOES
------------
A RULER that slides under a fixed mark in the centre, like a degree gauge: the
value is always read in the centre and what moves is the scale. It is driven
ONLY by pinch. Zooming in crops on whichever camera is in use. Going below 1x
switches to the ultra-wide. It is what any ordinary phone does.

The front camera is chosen with the rotate button, and there the bar starts at
1x because there is no wider camera underneath.

WHAT IT REPLACES, AND WHY
-------------------------
A pinch gesture that SWITCHED cameras, and a first attempt at a bar with a bead
that was dragged. It was worse than having nothing: there was no indicator, so
there was no way to know which camera you had ended up on; from the front camera
it jumped to the rear one by accident; and "switch camera" is not what a pinch
means to anybody.

1x is ALWAYS the main camera, not "whichever one is in use": if zoom meant a
different thing depending on where you were, it would mean nothing.

DIGITAL ZOOM
------------
The crop goes in the container and the magnification in the VideoOutput.
libcamera does not expose ScalerCrop on this pipeline handler -cam
--list-controls only gives Contrast and Gamma-, so the crop has to be done by
the application.

THE BAR IS ON A LOGARITHMIC SCALE
---------------------------------
On a linear one the whole useful travel piles up at the start: there is as much
visual difference between 1x and 2x as between 4x and 8x, and on a linear scale
the first would take up a seventh of the width of the second.

WHAT IS SPECIFIC TO THIS PHONE
------------------------------
That the ultra-wide is "camera@21". libcamera DOES NOT PUBLISH focal length or
field of view: only Model and Location are known, and there are TWO rear
cameras, so there is no way to deduce which one covers more. The order of the
list is no good either, because it changes between boots.

A NUMBER THAT IS NOT MEASURED
-----------------------------
factorGranAngular = 0.6. It is an ESTIMATE: it is obtained by photographing the
same scene with both rear cameras and comparing how much it shrinks, and on
trying it both came out black because the phone was face up. See
camara/medir-el-factor-del-gran-angular. Until it is measured, the "0.6x" that
the bar shows is not to be trusted; the camera CROSSOVER does work with any
value.

TWO BUGS FROM THE FIRST ATTEMPT, BOTH OF THEM MINE
--------------------------------------------------
"At 1x the ultra-wide shows up". Two chained causes:

  - The zoom comes out of an exponential, so at the 1x position it is worth
    0.99999998 and the "< 1.0" went into the ultra-wide branch. Now there is a
    margin, and a magnet that pins 1x exactly.
  - If the camera was BUSY the switch was skipped and WAS NEVER RETRIED.
    Starting up with the ultra-wide in use from a previous session, zoomPedido
    stayed at 1.0, there was no switch event, and nobody went back to the main
    camera. Now it is retried and it is also applied at startup.

"If I drag the bead it does not move properly, it stops halfway". The drag has
been removed: zoom is by pinch only, which is what was asked for. And the
animation on the scale has been removed too, since it made the image chase the
finger with a lag and gave the feeling of being stuck.

  - And a third one, which only turned up on looking at the app's log after
    "fixing it": plasma-camera RESTORES the camera from the previous session
    AFTER Component.onCompleted runs, so the startup correction arrived and was
    immediately overwritten. Now it is also corrected on a camera switch,
    wherever the switch comes from.

THE MAGNET, AND WHY IT IS RELATIVE
----------------------------------
The zoom sticks to the ultra-wide end stop and to the integers: 1x, 2x, 3x...
Those are the values people look for and the ones the ruler labels, so the
number and the mark always coincide.

The margin is RELATIVE -5%-, not absolute. A fixed one of 0.05 is clearly
noticeable at 1x, where it is 5%, and imperceptible at 8x, where it is worth
0.6%. In relative terms, snapping costs the same over the whole travel.

And with a DEAD ZONE: while inside the magnet the value stays pinned to the end
stop. Without that the number wobbles around 2.0 and does not give the feeling
of having snapped to anything, which was the complaint.

THE ROTATE BUTTON, WHICH THIS PATCH ALSO FIXES
----------------------------------------------
It called switchToNextCamera(), which walks through ALL the cameras: from the
main one it went to the ultra-wide and from there to the front one. Now that the
ultra-wide is reached with the zoom, that walk breaks the button: the
viewfinder's correction returns the ultra-wide to the main camera as soon as the
zoom is worth 1x, so the button bounced and NEVER got to the front camera. It
looked like an error when rotating.

Now it alternates rear <-> front directly, which is what the icon means now that
the ultra-wide has its own path.

